# Flutter Project Initializer (SSH Mode)

A bash script that clones a Flutter base/template repository, renames it into a
new project, applies the app name and bundle ID, sets the Flutter version via
FVM, and pushes the result to a fresh target repository — all over SSH.

## What it does

1. Prompts for the base template repo, new project name, app name, bundle ID,
   target repo, and Flutter version.
2. Validates:
   - Project name is `snake_case`.
   - Bundle ID is reverse-DNS style (e.g. `com.company.app`).
   - Both the base and target repos are SSH URLs (`git@github.com:...`).
   - SSH access to GitHub is working.
3. Clones the base repo (shallow, `--depth=1`) into `./temp_<project_name>`.
4. Strips the cloned repo's git history.
5. Updates `pubspec.yaml`'s `name:` field.
6. Applies the app name and bundle ID to Android and iOS:
   - Android: `android:label` in `AndroidManifest.xml`, `applicationId` in
     `build.gradle(.kts)`.
   - iOS: `CFBundleDisplayName` / `CFBundleName` in `Info.plist` (via
     `plutil`), `PRODUCT_BUNDLE_IDENTIFIER` in `project.pbxproj`.
7. Sets the Flutter version with [FVM](https://fvm.app) if installed.
8. Re-initializes git, commits, and pushes to the target repo over SSH.

## Requirements

- `git` with an SSH key added to your GitHub account
  ([docs](https://docs.github.com/en/authentication/connecting-to-github-with-ssh)).
- `bash`, `sed`.
- `plutil` (macOS only) for iOS display name updates — the script skips this
  step with a warning on Linux/CI runners where `plutil` isn't available.
- [FVM](https://fvm.app) (optional) if you want automatic Flutter version
  pinning.

## Usage

```bash
chmod +x init_flutter_project.sh
./init_flutter_project.sh
```

You'll be prompted for:

| Prompt              | Example                                  |
|----------------------|-------------------------------------------|
| Base Template Repo   | `git@github.com:yourorg/flutter_base.git` |
| Project Name         | `mfactory_flutter`                        |
| App Name             | `MFactory`                                |
| Bundle ID            | `com.mfactory.app`                        |
| Target Repo          | `git@github.com:yourorg/mfactory_flutter.git` |
| Flutter Version      | `3.24.0`                                  |

The target repo should already exist (even if empty) before running the
script.

## Why SSH-only?

This script intentionally refuses HTTPS remotes. Pushing to GitHub over HTTPS
requires a token embedded in the remote URL (e.g.
`https://x-access-token:<TOKEN>@github.com/owner/repo.git`) or a credential
helper — plain username/password auth was removed by GitHub. Using SSH avoids
this class of failure entirely, as long as your key is registered with
GitHub.

## CI usage: getting a token with the right permissions

This script itself only uses SSH, but if you're running it (or a variant of
it) inside CI — e.g. a `bootstrap.sh` step in GitHub Actions that also
*creates* the target repo before pushing — you'll need a **Personal Access
Token (PAT)**, since the workflow's default `GITHUB_TOKEN` is scoped only to
the repo the workflow runs in and can't create or push to other repos.

1. **Choose token type.** Use a classic PAT if the workflow needs to *create*
   new repos — fine-grained PATs can't grant "create repository" access for
   repos that don't exist yet.
2. **Generate it.** GitHub → [Settings → Developer settings → Personal access
   tokens → Tokens (classic)](https://github.com/settings/tokens) → Generate
   new token. Give it a name and expiration.
3. **Grant scopes.** Check `repo` (Full control of private repositories) —
   covers create, push, and admin on repos you own. For org-owned repos, also
   check `admin:org` → `read:org`, and authorize the token for SSO if the org
   enforces it.
4. **Save as a secret.** Repo → Settings → Secrets and variables → Actions →
   New repository secret. Name it e.g. `GH_PAT`. Never hardcode it in a
   script or commit it.
5. **Reference it in the workflow:**
   ```yaml
   env:
     GH_PAT: ${{ secrets.GH_PAT }}
   ```
6. **Use it in the push URL:**
   ```bash
   git remote add origin "https://x-access-token:${GH_PAT}@github.com/OWNER/REPO.git"
   ```
7. **If using `gh repo create`**, the `gh` CLI expects `GH_TOKEN` (or
   `GITHUB_TOKEN`) specifically — set `export GH_TOKEN="$GH_PAT"` before
   calling it.

**Note:** if the target repos live in an organization, the org may block
personal access tokens entirely unless explicitly allowed (Org Settings →
Third-party Access → Personal access tokens) — the token can look correctly
scoped and still get silently rejected if this isn't enabled.

## Rotating / updating the token

Classic PATs expire (max 90 days) or can be revoked, and the workflow will
start failing with the same `Invalid username or token` / `403` / `401`
error once that happens. To fix it:

1. **Generate a new token.** GitHub → Settings → Developer settings →
   Personal access tokens → Tokens (classic) → **Generate new token
   (classic)**. You can't reuse or un-expire an old token — this always
   creates a fresh value. Set an expiration (max 90 days) and check the
   `repo` scope.
2. **Copy the token immediately.** GitHub shows it only once, right after
   generation. If you navigate away before copying it, you'll have to
   generate another one.
3. **Open your repo's secrets.** Go to the repo where the workflow runs →
   Settings → Secrets and variables → Actions.
4. **Update the existing secret.** Find the secret your workflow reads
   (`TOKEN` or `GH_TOKEN` — check the `env:` block in your `.yml` file to see
   which name is actually live). Click it → **Update secret** → paste the
   new value → save. You don't need to delete and recreate it.
5. **Confirm org access if applicable.** If the target repo is under an
   organization, check Organization Settings → Third-party Access →
   Personal access tokens to make sure the new token is approved there —
   otherwise it can be silently rejected even though it looks valid.
6. **Re-run the workflow.** No script or workflow file changes are needed —
   the next run picks up the new secret value automatically.

> ⚠️ **Name mismatch check:** if your repo has more than one version of the
> bootstrap script in circulation, confirm the secret name you're updating
> matches what the *currently deployed* script reads. One version expects
> `TOKEN` + `USER_NAME`, another expects `GH_TOKEN` — updating the wrong one
> leaves the script reading an empty value and failing the same way.

## Troubleshooting

**`remote: Invalid username or token. Password authentication is not
supported for Git operations.`**
This means a push attempted plain HTTPS auth. If you're running this in CI
(e.g. GitHub Actions) rather than locally:
- Use an SSH deploy key added as a secret, and set up the SSH agent in the
  workflow before running this script, **or**
- If you must use HTTPS, embed a token explicitly:
  `git remote set-url origin https://x-access-token:${TOKEN}@github.com/owner/repo.git`
  instead of relying on the plain URL.

**`SSH not fully verified` warning**
Run `ssh -T git@github.com` manually and confirm you see "You've successfully
authenticated." Add your public key to GitHub if not
([Settings → SSH and GPG keys](https://github.com/settings/keys)).

**`Cannot access repo` on the base or target repo**
Confirm the repo exists, you have access, and the SSH URL is correct
(`git@github.com:owner/repo.git`, not the HTTPS form).

**iOS bundle ID / name not applied**
The `plutil` step only runs on macOS. On Linux CI runners, either post-process
`Info.plist` with a plist library, or accept the default and rename manually
in Xcode afterward.